{"name":"Lost Lake","hex":"#b5adb5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lost","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of lose"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having wandered from, or unable to find, the way.","In an unknown location; unable to be found."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lost"},{"term":"lake","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large, landlocked stretch of water or similar liquid.","A large amount of liquid."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An offering, sacrifice, gift.","Play; sport; game; fun; glee."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lake"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lost Lake, Mississippi","blocks":[{"text":"Lost Lake is an unincorporated community in Tunica County, Mississippi, United States."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lost_Lake%2C_Mississippi","wikidata":"Q6684093"},{"title":"Lost Lake, Wisconsin","blocks":[{"text":"Lost Lake is an unincorporated community in the towns of Calamus and Westford, Dodge County, Wisconsin, United States."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lost_Lake%2C_Wisconsin","wikidata":"Q14714518"},{"title":"Lost Lake (Hood River County, Oregon)","blocks":[{"text":"Lost Lake is a lake in Mount Hood National Forest 10.1 miles (16.2 km) northwest of Mount Hood in Hood River County in the U.S. state of Oregon. It is 21.4 miles (34.4 km) southwest of Hood River, a 26.4-mile (42.5 km) drive."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Famous_View_of_Lost_Lake_Mount_hood_in_the_distance.jpg/330px-Famous_View_of_Lost_Lake_Mount_hood_in_the_distance.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lost_Lake_(Hood_River_County%2C_Oregon)","wikidata":"Q6684102"},{"title":"Lake","blocks":[{"text":"A lake is often a naturally occurring, relatively large and fixed body of water on or near the Earth's surface. It is localized in a basin or interconnected basins surrounded by dry land. Lakes lie completely on land and are separate from the ocean, although they may be connected with the ocean by outflowing rivers. Lakes, like other bodies of water, are part of the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Most lakes are fresh water and account for almost all the world's surface freshwater, but some are salt lakes with salinities even higher than that of seawater. Lakes vary significantly in surface area and volume of water, but in total cover approximately 2.5 × 106 km2 (less than 2%) of the Earth's surface."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Lake_Idro_Italy_2005-08-16.jpg/330px-Lake_Idro_Italy_2005-08-16.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake","wikidata":"Q23397"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Lost_Lake_Resort.jpg/120px-Lost_Lake_Resort.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Lost_Lake_Resort.jpg/250px-Lost_Lake_Resort.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Lost_Lake_Resort.jpg/120px-Lost_Lake_Resort.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALost_Lake_Resort.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Rowboat_at_Lost_Lake%2C_Oreogon.jpg/120px-Rowboat_at_Lost_Lake%2C_Oreogon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Rowboat_at_Lost_Lake%2C_Oreogon.jpg/250px-Rowboat_at_Lost_Lake%2C_Oreogon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/